##Installation and Setup

1. Clone the Repository

git clone https://github.com/shashidhargenna/code-review-agent.git
cd code-review-agent

2. Create a Virtual Environment

python -m venv venv

Activate it:

macOS/Linux

source venv/bin/activate

Windows

venv\Scripts\activate

3. Install Dependencies

pip install -r requirements.txt

4. Configure Environment Variables

## Create a .env file in the project root:

OPENAI_API_KEY=your_openai_api_key
GITHUB_TOKEN=your_github_personal_access_token

The GitHub token should have the permissions required to read Pull Requests and write to the source repository branch.

Keep your API keys private. Never commit your .env file to GitHub.

Running the Application

The application supports two execution methods.

Option 1: Command-Line Interface

Run:

## python -m app.main

Enter a GitHub Pull Request URL when prompted.

The application will:

1. Fetch modified Python files.
2. Run Flake8 code analysis.
3. Generate refactored code.
4. Display the proposed changes.
5. Ask for human approval.
6. If rejected, collect reviewer feedback and generate another version.
7. If approved, commit the refactored code to the PR source branch.

Option 2: FastAPI

Start the API server:

python -m uvicorn app.main:api --reload

Open the interactive API documentation:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs

API Endpoints

1. GitHub Webhook

Endpoint:

POST /api/webhooks/github

Starts the code review workflow using the Pull Request URL.

## Example request:

{
  "action": "opened",
  "pull_request": {
    "html_url": "https://github.com/owner/repository/pull/1"
  }
}

The response includes a thread_id and the generated refactored code.

2. Approve or Reject Review

Endpoint:

POST /api/reviews/{thread_id}/approve

Submits the human approval decision or rejection feedback.

## Example approval request:

{
  "approved": true,
  "feedback": ""
}

## Example rejection request:

{
  "approved": false,
  "feedback": "Please improve variable names and add clearer docstrings."
}

The workflow resumes from its saved checkpoint using the original thread ID.

Human-in-the-Loop Workflow

Human approval is implemented using LangGraph’s interrupt() functionality.

When the refactoring node generates improved code, the workflow pauses at the human approval node.

* If approved: The workflow proceeds to the GitHub update node.
* If rejected: Reviewer feedback is stored in the workflow state, and execution returns to the refactoring node.

The LLM uses the feedback to generate an improved version, after which the workflow pauses for approval again.

SQLite checkpointing allows the workflow to resume using its original thread ID.

Testing

Run the complete test suite:

python -m pytest -v

The tests cover:

* GitHub Pull Request fetching.
* Linting and syntax-error isolation.
* LLM refactoring behavior.
* GitHub commit operations using mocked API responses.
* Approval protection.
* Workflow state.
* Workflow compilation.
* Rejection and feedback-based refactoring.

